new_candidates.body:
Drake's SDFormat parser doesn't always use a `Parser` model name prefix, or
the names that auto-renaming chose. It names models included from URDF/MJCF
files, and resolves world-scope references, with libsdformat's names instead.
References then either throw or, if an earlier parse already added a model
instance with the looked-up name, silently resolve into that instance.

Observed on master at 0b3db8d7c5 (Linux, opt build). The output is identical
with #25125 applied.

**Repro.** This program compiles as is against Drake. Each case uses a fresh
plant.

```cpp
#include <exception>
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

#include <fmt/format.h>

#include "drake/common/temp_directory.h"
#include "drake/multibody/parsing/parser.h"
#include "drake/multibody/plant/multibody_plant.h"

using drake::multibody::ModelInstanceIndex;
using drake::multibody::MultibodyPlant;
using drake::multibody::Parser;

namespace {

// Parses `contents`, printing any exception rather than throwing it.
void Parse(Parser* parser, const std::string& contents) {
  try {
    parser->AddModelsFromString(contents, "sdf");
  } catch (const std::exception& e) {
    std::cout << "  threw: " << e.what() << "\n";
  }
}

// Prints which bodies each joint named "j" connects.
void PrintJoints(const MultibodyPlant<double>& plant) {
  for (ModelInstanceIndex i(0); i < plant.num_model_instances(); ++i) {
    if (!plant.HasJointNamed("j", i)) continue;
    const auto& joint = plant.GetJointByName("j", i);
    std::cout << "  joint j: " << joint.parent_body().name() << " in '"
              << plant.GetModelInstanceName(joint.parent_body().model_instance())
              << "' -> " << joint.child_body().name() << " in '"
              << plant.GetModelInstanceName(joint.child_body().model_instance())
              << "'\n";
  }
}

}  // namespace

int main() {
  const std::string urdf = drake::temp_directory() + "/u.urdf";
  std::ofstream(urdf) << R"(<robot name='u'>
  <link name='R'/>
  <link name='B'/>
  <joint name='RB' type='fixed'><parent link='R'/><child link='B'/></joint>
</robot>)";
  const std::string model = fmt::format(R"(<sdf version='1.12'>
  <model name='m'>
    <include merge='false'><uri>file://{}</uri><name>u</name></include>
    <link name='A'/>
    <joint name='j' type='fixed'><parent>A</parent><child>u::B</child></joint>
  </model>
</sdf>)",
                                        urdf);
  const std::string world = R"(<sdf version='1.12'>
  <world name='w'>
    <model name='foo'><link name='L'/></model>
    <joint name='j' type='fixed'><parent>world</parent><child>foo::L</child></joint>
  </world>
</sdf>)";
  const std::string earlier_u =
      "<sdf version='1.12'><model name='u'><link name='B'/></model></sdf>";
  const std::string earlier_foo =
      "<sdf version='1.12'><model name='foo'><link name='L'/></model></sdf>";

  std::cout << "Case 1: prefix + included URDF\n";
  {
    MultibodyPlant<double> plant(0.0);
    Parser parser(&plant, "pre");
    Parse(&parser, model);
    PrintJoints(plant);
  }
  std::cout << "Case 1, after an earlier parse added pre::m::u\n";
  {
    MultibodyPlant<double> plant(0.0);
    Parser earlier(&plant, "pre::m");
    Parse(&earlier, earlier_u);
    Parser parser(&plant, "pre");
    Parse(&parser, model);
    PrintJoints(plant);
  }
  std::cout << "Case 2: prefix + world-scope reference\n";
  {
    MultibodyPlant<double> plant(0.0);
    Parser parser(&plant, "pre");
    Parse(&parser, world);
    PrintJoints(plant);
  }
  std::cout << "Case 2, after an earlier parse added foo\n";
  {
    MultibodyPlant<double> plant(0.0);
    Parser earlier(&plant);
    Parse(&earlier, earlier_foo);
    Parser parser(&plant, "pre");
    Parse(&parser, world);
    PrintJoints(plant);
  }
  std::cout << "Case 3: auto-renaming + included URDF, parsed twice\n";
  {
    MultibodyPlant<double> plant(0.0);
    Parser parser(&plant);
    parser.SetAutoRenaming(true);
    Parse(&parser, model);
    Parse(&parser, model);
    PrintJoints(plant);
  }
  std::cout << "Case 4: auto-renaming + world-scope reference, after an "
               "earlier parse added foo\n";
  {
    MultibodyPlant<double> plant(0.0);
    Parser earlier(&plant);
    Parse(&earlier, earlier_foo);
    Parser parser(&plant);
    parser.SetAutoRenaming(true);
    Parse(&parser, world);
    PrintJoints(plant);
  }
  return 0;
}
```

**Output** (every case was run, so all of these are observed, not inferred):

```
Case 1: prefix + included URDF
  threw: GetModelInstanceByName(): There is no model instance named 'pre::m::u'. The current model instances are 'DefaultModelInstance', 'WorldModelInstance', 'm::u', 'pre::m'.
Case 1, after an earlier parse added pre::m::u
  joint j: A in 'pre::m' -> B in 'pre::m::u'
Case 2: prefix + world-scope reference
  threw: GetModelInstanceByName(): There is no model instance named 'foo'. The current model instances are 'DefaultModelInstance', 'WorldModelInstance', 'pre::foo'.
Case 2, after an earlier parse added foo
  joint j: world in 'WorldModelInstance' -> L in 'foo'
Case 3: auto-renaming + included URDF, parsed twice
  threw: error: Child frame with name[u::B] specified by joint with name[j] not found in model with name[m].
  joint j: A in 'm' -> B in 'm::u'
Case 4: auto-renaming + world-scope reference, after an earlier parse added foo
  joint j: world in 'WorldModelInstance' -> L in 'foo'
```

**Expected vs. actual:**
- **Case 1** (prefix + included URDF).
  - Expected: model instances `pre::m` and `pre::m::u`, and joint `j` welds
    `A` to `pre::m::u`'s `B`.
  - Actual: it throws. The include was named `m::u`.
- **Case 1 after an earlier `pre::m::u`.**
  - Expected: as above, or an error.
  - Actual: no error, and joint `j` welds `A` to the *earlier* model's `B`.
- **Case 2** (prefix + world-scope reference).
  - Expected: joint `j` welds the world to `pre::foo`'s `L`.
  - Actual: it throws.
- **Case 2 after an earlier `foo`.**
  - Expected: as above, or an error.
  - Actual: no error, and joint `j` welds the world to the *earlier* `foo`'s
    `L`.
- **Case 3** (auto-renaming + included URDF, parsed twice).
  - Expected: a second copy, `m_1` with `m_1::u`.
  - Actual: the second parse throws. The include was renamed `m::u_1`, so
    libsdformat's interface model is named `u_1` and can't be found as `u`.
    The plant keeps only the first copy's joint.
- **Case 4** (auto-renaming + world-scope reference, after an earlier `foo`).
  - Expected: joint `j` welds the world to the renamed `foo_1`'s `L`.
  - Actual: no error, and it welds the *earlier* `foo`'s `L`.

**Where:**
- `ParseNestedInterfaceModel()` names an included model from
  `include.AbsoluteParentName()`, which has no prefix. For a merge include it
  calls `GetOrCreateModelInstanceByName()` with that name.
- `GetResolvedModelInstanceAndLocalName()` applies no prefix at world scope
  and doesn't check that the model instance came from the current parse. It is
  used through `GetBodyByLinkSpecificationName()` for ordinary joints, and by
  `AddFrameFromSpecification()`.
- `parsing::GetScopedFrameByNameMaybe()` has the same problem.

#25125 adds a "current parse only" check, `FindSourceModelInstance()`, for
frame names in `drake:` tags and in joints with `<drake:independent_frames/>`,
and for a `<frame>` whose `attached_to` names a model. It doesn't cover the
lookups above.

When a `<model>`'s canonical link is in a URDF or MJCF model included into it
(merged or not), Drake fixes the model's implicit `__model__` frame to the
world. SDFormat attaches that frame to the canonical link. Any frame attached
to the model is then world-fixed too, whether it names the model or
`__model__` in `attached_to` or is attached to it by default.

**Impact:** such a frame stays at its initial world pose while the included
model moves.

Observed on master at 0b3db8d7c5 (Linux, opt build). The output is identical
with #25125 applied.

**Repro.** This program compiles as is against Drake.

```cpp
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

#include <fmt/format.h>

#include "drake/common/temp_directory.h"
#include "drake/math/rigid_transform.h"
#include "drake/multibody/parsing/parser.h"
#include "drake/multibody/plant/multibody_plant.h"

using drake::math::RigidTransformd;
using drake::multibody::MultibodyPlant;
using drake::multibody::Parser;

int main() {
  const std::string urdf = drake::temp_directory() + "/u.urdf";
  std::ofstream(urdf) << R"(<robot name='u'>
  <link name='R'/>
  <link name='B'/>
  <joint name='RB' type='fixed'><parent link='R'/><child link='B'/></joint>
</robot>)";
  struct Variant {
    const char* description;
    const char* merge;
    const char* canonical_link;  // As an attribute of <model name='w'>.
  };
  for (const Variant& variant :
       {Variant{"include", "false", ""},
        Variant{"merged include", "true", ""},
        Variant{"include, canonical_link='u::B'", "false",
                " canonical_link='u::B'"}}) {
    MultibodyPlant<double> plant(0.0);
    Parser(&plant).AddModelsFromString(
        fmt::format(R"(<sdf version='1.12'>
  <model name='w'{}>
    <include merge='{}'><uri>file://{}</uri><name>u</name></include>
    <frame name='F'/>
  </model>
</sdf>)",
                    variant.canonical_link, variant.merge, urdf),
        "sdf");
    const auto& frame_F = plant.GetFrameByName("F");
    std::cout << variant.description << ": F is attached to body '"
              << frame_F.body().name() << "'\n";
    plant.Finalize();
    auto context = plant.CreateDefaultContext();
    plant.SetFreeBodyPose(context.get(), plant.GetBodyByName("R"),
                          RigidTransformd(Eigen::Vector3d(1, 2, 3)));
    const Eigen::Vector3d p_WF = frame_F.CalcPoseInWorld(*context).translation();
    std::cout << "  after moving R to (1, 2, 3), F is at (" << p_WF.x() << ", "
              << p_WF.y() << ", " << p_WF.z() << ")\n";
  }
  return 0;
}
```

**Output:**

```
include: F is attached to body 'world'
  after moving R to (1, 2, 3), F is at (0, 0, 0)
merged include: F is attached to body 'world'
  after moving R to (1, 2, 3), F is at (0, 0, 0)
include, canonical_link='u::B': F is attached to body 'world'
  after moving R to (1, 2, 3), F is at (0, 0, 0)
```

**Expected:**
- `F` is attached to the model's canonical link. That's `R`, the included
  model's first link, for the first two variants, and `B` for
  `canonical_link='u::B'`.
- So after `R` moves to (1, 2, 3), `F` is at (1, 2, 3) in every variant. `B`
  is welded to `R` at its origin.

**Actual:** `F` is attached to the world and stays at (0, 0, 0).

**Cause:** for a link that a custom parser loaded (an "interface link"),
`sdf::Model::CanonicalLinkAndRelativeName()` returns a null link and only the
link's name (sdformat `src/Model.cc`, the interface-link branches).
`AddModelsFromSpecification()` then takes its branch for a model with no
usable canonical link, which fixes `__model__` to the world at the model's
pose.

**Suggested fix:** in that branch, when the canonical link is null but its
name isn't empty, resolve the name in the plant and attach `__model__` to
that body. #25125 marks the spot with a TODO.

**Interaction with #25125:**
- Naming such a model's frame is an error, whether by the model's name, as
  `w::__model__`, or as `__model__`. That applies in `<drake:parent>`,
  `<drake:child>`, `<drake:bushing_frameA>`, `<drake:bushing_frameC>`, and a
  `<joint>` with `<drake:independent_frames/>`.
- A frame attached to such a model (like `F` above) still has the problem:
  - A `<drake:joint>` or bushing that names it silently gets the world-fixed
    frame.
  - A `<joint>` with `<drake:independent_frames/>` reports an error, because
    the frame's link doesn't match the one SDFormat resolves.
